NAME
  mux - tmux-like terminal multiplexer for OpenComputers (on OpenOS)

SYNOPSIS
  mux [attach | new CMD... | list | kill-session]

DESCRIPTION
  `mux` runs one terminal session that holds many terminal panes, and lets you
  detach from it and re-attach from any terminal on the same machine. Programs
  in the panes keep running (they keep producing output and pumping every event
  except the keyboard) while no client is attached, and are waiting for you when
  you attach again.

  There is exactly one session per machine; it lives in the machine's Lua heap
  under the global `_G.mux_session`. Only one client can be attached at a time.
  Running `mux` in a second terminal steals the screen from the first: the old
  client prints "terminal was taken over" and exits.

  Each pane runs a full shell (or the command you asked for) in its own process,
  confined to its own region of the screen. Both line-based programs (that use
  tty/term) and programs that talk to the GPU directly are clipped to the pane's
  bounds. Mouse input (touch/drag/drop/scroll) is mapped to whichever pane
  covers the clicked point, so mouse applications work per-pane.

  A session is a set of windows; each window is a binary split tree of panes.
  `mux` composites the panes onto the physical screen and draws a status line
  along the bottom row. While attached, `^B` is the command prefix (like tmux's
  `^A`/`^B`): the status line shows `mux+` while a prefix is armed.

COMMANDS
  attach
    Attach to the session, creating it (with one shell pane) if it is missing.
    This is the default: a bare `mux` does the same thing.

  new CMD...
    Run CMD in a new window. If no session exists, a new one is created running
    CMD and "session created" is printed; otherwise CMD opens as an extra window
    in the existing session.

  list
    List the windows of the running session as `<n>: <title> (<p> pane(s))`,
    marking the active one with `[active]`. Fails if no session is running.

  kill-session
    End the session and free all panes and their video buffers. Safe to run
    while a client is pumping: that client notices the session is gone.

  --help
    Print a short usage summary and the command keys, then exit.

COMMAND KEYS (after the ^B prefix)
  %              split the active pane into left and right halves
  "              split the active pane into top and bottom halves
  x              kill the active pane
  &              kill the active window (all its panes)
  c              new window (shell)
  n / p          next / previous window
  0-9            jump to a window (0 selects window 1, tmux style)
  o              cycle the active pane within the current window
  h j k l        focus the pane to the left / down / up / right
  arrows         resize the active pane's split
  z              toggle zoom (the active pane fills the whole work area)
  w              rename the window (a `title:` prompt appears on the status line)
  d              detach: the client quits, but the session and every pane keep running
  q              kill the session and exit
  ^B             cancel the prefix
  ^C             send an interrupt to the active pane

  A split needs the pane to be at least 3 cells wide or tall and enough free
  video memory; if not, `mux` reports the reason on the status line. Splitting
  inside a zoomed window un-zooms it first, like tmux.

STATUS LINE
  The bottom row shows, from left to right: the `mux`/`mux+` hint, the list of
  windows (`n:title`, the active one highlighted), and on the right an optional
  `[zoom]` marker, the clock, the screen size, and free machine memory and free
  video memory as percentages. Each memory percentage is green over 50%, yellow
  over 25%, red below that. When there are more windows than fit they are
  truncated with `...`, but the selected window is always kept visible. Any
  session error (for example running out of video memory) is shown as `! ...`
  for a few seconds.

NOTES
  Detaching (or the attached `mux` process dying for any reason other than the
  machine rebooting) leaves the panes running: a watchdog re-parents their
  wake-ups to the init process so they never freeze, and they start receiving
  every signal except keyboard input until you attach again.

  `mux` keeps its own modifier-key state because the keyboard handler that
  OpenOS's boot script registers only runs under init; this is what lets the
  `^B` prefix arm and what lets a pane read `^D` as end-of-file.

  There is no scrollback buffer (panes show only their live screen), only one
  session per machine and one attached client at a time, and no `mux`-inside-`mux`
  protection.

EXAMPLES
  mux
    Attach to the session, starting it if there isn't one yet.

  mux new edit /tmp/log
    Open `edit /tmp/log` in a new window (creating the session if needed).

  mux list
    Show the windows of the running session.

  ^B %
    While attached, split the active pane left and right.

  ^B d
    Detach, leaving every pane running for a later `mux` to re-attach to.
